# Gate step 1285-vz27 — data, not code (order 1072-b7eq).
#
# WHAT IT GUARDS. tillandsias-plan set-field's positional scan skipped `i += 2`
# for ANY token starting with `--`, which is two defects in one line:
#
#   an UNKNOWN flag silently ate the next token, so a typo — or a flag borrowed
#   from a sibling subcommand — vanished along with its argument; and
#
#   a VALUELESS flag (--append/--replace/--backfill) ate the token after it,
#   normally the NEXT FLAG'S NAME, shifting every later token left and promoting
#   some unrelated string to the positional VALUE.
#
# Both then WROTE, and printed a success-shaped line. Measured on macneo
# 2026-09-19: a p1 row's next_action was overwritten with the literal
# `tlatoanis-macbook-neo`, and `tillandsias-plan check` reported ok, ids unique,
# references sound — every validator passing over a field that had just been
# replaced by a hostname. The prose the field existed to carry was never written
# and nothing said so.
#
# IT HAPPENED TWICE, THE SECOND TIME HERE. On 2026-09-20 this host ran a
# `--dry-run` probe against set-field to read the status vocabulary; --dry-run is
# not a set-field flag, it was absorbed in silence, and a note fragment was
# written against a row that was not being edited. Caught only by looking at
# `git status` afterwards. A caller who trusts the success line cannot tell that
# from a correct write — which is why this guards the LEDGER and is not a
# usability nit.
#
# PRE-FIX / POST-FIX, measured against a throwaway ledger copy:
#   pre-fix  set-field <ref> notes "probe" --append --host H --ts T --dry-run
#            -> "[ts H] probe (…fragment.yaml)"   fragments 1266 -> 1267
#   post-fix -> "error: unknown flag '--dry-run' … REFUSED before any write"
#               fragments unchanged
#
# EVERY ARM WRITES TO A COPY. The fixture copies plan/ to a temp dir and passes
# --index; the real ledger is never touched. A test for a tool whose defect is an
# unwanted write must not be able to produce one.
#
# ARM 5 IS THE MUTANT AND IS OPT-IN. Restoring the permissive parse needs a
# ~40s rebuild and patches a source file, which a gate step must not do to a
# working tree. It is skipped BY NAME rather than silently, and the pre-fix
# measurement above is recorded on the row instead of asserted every run. Set
# TILLANDSIAS_SET_FIELD_MUTANT=1 to drive it.
#
# SECOND REGIME (order 1302-7j8p): NONE REQUIRED, and this is the whole answer
# rather than a deferral. The fixture's default path shells out only to
# cp date dirname env grep head ls mkdir mktemp printf rm tail wc — POSIX
# spellings with no GNU-only name and no GNU-only flag, all of them already
# exercised on every platform by steps 411 and 413 — plus the plan binary THIS
# REPO BUILDS, which is the subject under test. There is no external tool whose
# absence or dialect could change the verdict. The opt-in mutant arm
# additionally needs perl and cargo, and skips by name when they are missing.
# If that list ever grows, this step needs a recorded run on a second regime
# before it stays in --check.
#
# COST, MEASURED HERE 2026-09-20: 1.65 / 1.58 s. It copies a 95k-line ledger and
# its 1263 fragments to a temp dir and runs the built binary six times. No
# compile in the default path, no container, no network.
#
# STEP_SCRIPT is a LITERAL path on purpose: a binding assembled from a variable
# is invisible to every name-based scan, including the bound-or-retired guard
# (1063-nraf). Keep it a plain string.
STEP_DESC="Checking set-field refuses an unknown flag instead of absorbing it and writing (1285-vz27)"
STEP_SCRIPT="scripts/test-set-field-refuses-unknown-flags.sh"
STEP_ERROR="tillandsias-plan set-field can again absorb an unknown flag, consume the next option's value as the field value, WRITE it to the ledger and print success (1285-vz27) — read which arm failed above; ARM 1 failing means the ledger-corrupting path is open"
STEP_OK="set-field refuses unknown flags before writing"
STEP_SECOND_REGIME="macneo 2026-09-22 darwin ok:set-field-unknown-flags:6/6"
